WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.630
DAN MCGRATH: Hi,
I'm Dan McGrath.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.700
I'm the product lead for
Google's NoSQL databases.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.450
And I'm here in this session
to give you some insight

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:17.110
into modernizing your HBase
workloads with Clout Bigtable.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.940
But before we get
into that, I want

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.718
to give you a quick introduction
into Cloud Bigtable,

00:00:21.718 --> 00:00:24.010
and if you're already familiar
with it, talk about some

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:27.298
of the recent additions
to the service.

00:00:27.298 --> 00:00:29.090
And to kick that off,
I'm going to give you

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.760
a little bit of trivia that you
might not have heard before.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.110
Because this year,
2020, marks 15 years

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.810
of Cloud Bigtable being in
production here at Google.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.140
We've spent a lot of time
in that 15 years optimizing

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.560
Bigtable from the experience
of running everything

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.180
from small zonal workloads to
our largest and most demanding

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.860
workloads with deployments
spanning the globe.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.720
Here is a diagram from
a design doc from one

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.050
of those very first deployments
of Cloud Bigtable showing

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.230
a replicated system
across three regions.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.950
It was the following
year, in 2006,

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.100
that Google released the
Bigtable white paper, leading

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.680
to the development of HBase.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.810
After beating
production for 10 years,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.680
Cloud Bigtable was released
so companies outside of Google

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.630
could benefit from the same
technology used here at Google.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.880
And to make it easier
for developers,

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.370
HBase-compatible client
libraries backed by Bigtable

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.960
were also released.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.540
Now Cloud Bigtable is a fully
managed NoSQL document service

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.810
for use cases where low
latency, random access data,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.490
scalability, and reliability
are very critical.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.500
It seamlessly scales
from your first gigabyte

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.680
to data sets that are
at petabyte scales.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.580
It can handle millions
of queries per second.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.380
And as a fully
managed service, it

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.590
allows your team to be
freed up from database

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.930
and infrastructure
management tasks,

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.650
allowing them to dedicate their
time to where the business can

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.150
add value.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.539
It's completely compatible
with the existing Apache HBase

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.530
setup, enabling seamless
migrations for existing HBase

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.302
workloads.

00:02:15.302 --> 00:02:21.710
Now Bigtable offers you a
database that provides speed,

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.250
scalability, a fully
managed foundation,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.030
and integration in the Google
Cloud and Apache ecosystem

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.760
as its core base.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.560
On the speed front,
it does give you

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.990
consistent 99th percentile
low latency for random access

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.070
reads and writes.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.680
It's the same
technology that powers

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.040
a range of billion-user
Google services.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.430
And it's proven in applications
with millions of database

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.250
accesses per second.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:55.850
It easily scales to match
user needs with no downtime.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:59.660
It's a perfect fit for
real-time storage and analysis

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.580
of huge amounts of data.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.790
One of the really nice
things about Cloud Bigtable

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.310
is it gives you
consistent performance

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.720
as your workload grows.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.370
It can be easily scaled by
simply adding nodes or even

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.980
removing nodes within the UI
or command line interface.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.570
It offers seamless
cluster scaling.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.000
So dynamically adding
and removing these nodes

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.090
can be done without restarts.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.660
And the storage system
automatically scales as well.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.950
So you only pay for the storage
that you are actively using.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:38.520
Through the separation of
processing and storage,

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.160
Cloud Bigtable is able to
automatically configure

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:46.440
throughput by adjusting the
association of nodes and data.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.790
In the rebalancing
example in the middle,

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.810
if node A is
experiencing heavy load,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.210
the routing layer can
move some of the traffic

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:59.260
to a less heavily loaded node,
improving overall performance.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.170
Resizing, in the third
example, comes into play

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.830
when a node is added, again
ensuring balanced load

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:10.000
across your nodes, ensuring
best overall throughput

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.620
of the system.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.250
It also offers zero-touch
failover for high availability

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.010
and a range of other use cases.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:21.560
Bigtable enables you to write
once to a single replicate

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.130
and have it automatically
replicated everywhere

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.080
with eventual consistency.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.440
There are no manual steps to
ensure consistency, repair

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.620
data, or synchronize
writes and deletes.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.620
It gives you the ability to
have a configurable topology

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.280
and policies on how to
route data, both at the read

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.280
and write side, to
different replicates.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.800
Common use case for
the replication system

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.650
are high availability for
your live serving apps,

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.080
or perhaps workload
isolation, where

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.740
you have both user serving
and batch workloads running

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.850
against the same database.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:57.610
It's also great for
a use case where

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.420
you want latency
optimization to write locally

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.730
to a replicate closer
to you, but then

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:05.860
be able to read that
data everywhere,

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.890
perhaps in some centralized
replica where all your batch

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:12.038
processing happens.

00:05:12.038 --> 00:05:15.680
So let's quickly talk about
some of the recent highlights.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.400
Now on the fully managed
side, Cloud Bigtable

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.600
integrates into Cloud IAM for
user access and authentication.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.470
At the end of 2019, we
announced table-level IAM,

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.040
allowing you to have more
granular rules on who can

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.230
access data within clusters.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.550
We also announced
multi-region replication

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.490
to enable you to replicate
data across the globe

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:43.430
as well as a host of new
regions that rolled out

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:46.290
to support Cloud Bigtable.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.930
Earlier this year,
in 2020, we also

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.900
announced single-node
production instances.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.470
So historically
with Cloud Bigtable,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.790
you were required
to have three nodes

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:01.410
to be able to be supported by
the service level agreement.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:05.340
Now you can have a single node
supported by the same SLA that

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.470
gives you 99.9% availability
for single-cluster routing

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.980
or 99.99% availability
for multi-cluster routing.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.830
Something that I'm really
excited about that we're just

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:22.220
announcing now, in July
2020, is the addition

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.750
of managed backups.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.440
So manage backups gives you
a fully automated method

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.050
to back up the tables
within your cluster

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.090
and then very
quickly restore them,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.670
either into the same
instance, or perhaps you

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:37.880
want to restore them
into a development or QA

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:41.420
cluster for ease of testing.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.660
Now let's have a look at how
you can think about modernizing

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:49.800
both your existing and new HBase
workloads with Cloud Bigtable.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.670
I like to think about it in
terms of two different paths.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:57.443
One is the backward-looking path
of why you want to modernize.

00:06:57.443 --> 00:06:58.860
And this is where
you're currently

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:02.030
struggling to maintain the
availability of your system.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:04.410
Perhaps you've had
durability issues.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:07.200
Or perhaps performance,
when scaling the system,

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.380
has become toilsome
for your team.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:11.970
And so an example
of this is where

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:15.900
you're struggling with stability
at scale or during peak load

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.920
events.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:20.580
Or you consider the operational
load on your SRE and DevOps

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.520
teams too distracting
from the overall core work

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.020
that you're trying to do.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.460
Perhaps the overhead of
managing the replication system,

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:32.910
repairs, and the data
garbage collection system

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.460
has also become to
toilsome for your team.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.350
Cloud Bigtable gives
you a great way

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.800
to automate away a
lot of those troubles.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.160
The other path where people
typically look at modernization

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.125
is forward-looking.

00:07:45.125 --> 00:07:46.500
So they want to
move up the stack

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.370
and have their development teams
working on something that's

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.200
more focused on
the differentiators

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.330
that the company offers.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.700
They want to spend
less time and money

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:00.180
managing the complexities
of a distributed database

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.810
and have more time to focus
on what was not previously

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.222
possible to prioritize.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.330
The really good
thing about looking

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:12.670
at Cloud Bigtable
with HBase is you'll

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:18.340
find most of the concepts map
very seamlessly between the two

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:19.857
systems.

00:08:19.857 --> 00:08:21.940
And that's obviously because
of the shared history

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:26.240
both Bigtable and HBase have had
for a long period of time now.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.320
So all the fundamentals are
going to be the same for you.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.900
If you know how to
design schemas on HBase,

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.900
you're going to be pretty
good at designing them

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.380
on Cloud Bigtable.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.380
Row keys, column families,
column qualifiers,

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:41.919
cells, they're all going to
be the same on Cloud Bigtable.

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:44.620
Even the cases where there are
some differences in the feature

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.560
sets that are available,
such as namespaces,

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.780
you will find ways
to simulate them

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:55.330
within Cloud Bigtable using
things like row key prefixes.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.560
Now some of the
terminology you're used to,

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.080
such as regions, are going
to be slightly different.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.287
We call them tablets
in Cloud Bigtable--

00:09:03.287 --> 00:09:04.120
very similar though.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:06.760
The really good thing
though is all those concerns

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.420
are automated and
fully managed for you.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.430
So you don't really need to
worry about them anymore.

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:14.740
Similarly, things like
snapshots, that's now

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:17.710
available as managed backups.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:19.820
And tasks like compaction--

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.120
so you can't configure that,
but it is automatically

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.283
managed for you.

00:09:23.283 --> 00:09:24.700
So there's no
maintenance work you

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:28.700
need to do on the compaction
and garbage collection sides.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:32.680
All in all, Cloud Bigtable
supports HBase client libraries

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:35.350
from 1.0 through to 1.3.

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:37.990
So you should find the
development environment

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:41.140
very familiar for you.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:44.180
Now if you look at the
architecture of Cloud Bigtable,

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.420
you'll see a lot of
similarities here as well.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:51.320
For replicating data,
replication to other clusters

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.570
can send different types of
traffic to different clusters.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.690
So we have a way
to set up policies

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.760
through a thing called
app profiles that

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.390
talk about how failover happens
and how you want to read--

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:06.440
how you want to route your
reads and writes on the system.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:09.050
Scaling is very simple
in Cloud Bigtable.

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:11.150
It's as simple as
adding nodes or even

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:15.830
removing nodes within the UI
or command line interface.

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:19.220
A table is automatically
sharded into blocks

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:20.460
of rows called tablets.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.410
And this is all managed
behind the scenes.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:25.490
We use a bunch of
heuristics around total data

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:28.970
size and traffic
to do this for you.

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:32.470
One of the really interesting
things is on the storage side.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:36.020
This is it automatically
scaling storage system as well,

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.950
where they're stored
encrypted, at rest on disk.

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:42.790
And even if you're only
running a single node of Cloud

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:44.560
Bigtable, rest
assured that your data

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:48.180
is durable across
multiple machines.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.270
Now when you're looking
at migrating your existing

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:53.880
workloads into Cloud
Bigtable, the first step

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:57.665
is always around preparation
and planning for the migration.

00:10:57.665 --> 00:10:59.040
So typically, you
want to-- going

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.620
to do a deep dive into
the different use cases

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.480
that you have running on HBase.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.340
And perhaps you'll go
down a different path

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.650
for different
types of use cases.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:10.410
It's really good to build up an
understanding of Cloud Bigtable

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:12.810
and where some of the
concepts and capabilities

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.680
might differ than what
the alternatives are.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.950
So you'll want to map concepts,
schema design, queries,

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.140
and how you're thinking about
data consistency from HBase

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.300
to Cloud Bigtable.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.550
Now there are some
slight differences in

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:30.090
supported functionality between
HBase and Cloud Bigtable.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:31.600
We have documentation on this.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:34.020
So I encourage you to read
through the documentation

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:36.630
and have an understanding of
where those differences may

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:40.690
lay early in the process.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.690
Now depending on
the use case, you

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.510
could end up going down a
variety of different paths.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.130
Some of them you will
find incredibly easy.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.957
Some of them might require
some more planning upfront.

00:11:50.957 --> 00:11:53.040
So we're going to look at
the four different types

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.880
of workloads and each of the
different migration strategies.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.340
And we'll walk through them all.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:03.230
So let's start with batch
load and fast serve.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.940
In this specific
migration path, we only

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:07.710
have a single writing component.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.270
And this simplifies
things a lot.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:14.700
So this is a batch job that
might be refreshing data

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.950
periodically, such as models
that you're serving up.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:21.270
But then you're using HBase
to have that fast serve

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:25.230
capacity for that data that's
being written into the system.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.330
Now the obvious
first step here is

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:31.050
to update the batch job to write
into both HBase and Bigtable

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:32.520
at the same time.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.910
Once this is done, now you
can spend some time verifying

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:38.280
the batch job to
make sure that it's

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.030
writing the same information
into both systems.

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:45.990
After this is done, we can roll
out a change to the API layer

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:49.410
that is reading from
HBase to also support

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:52.092
reading from Bigtable.

00:12:52.092 --> 00:12:54.300
Once you're happy with the
performance of the system,

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.480
you can cut over to reading
purely from Bigtable

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.200
and then update your batch jobs
to stop writing into Bigtable.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:02.832
At that point in
time, once you're

00:13:02.832 --> 00:13:04.290
happy with the
performance, you can

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.660
start decommissioning
your old system.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.660
Now, when downtime
is acceptable,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.880
this really simplifies things.

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:19.570
And this is by far the most
common migration path we see.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.180
It works for all types
of HBase workloads

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.550
as long as you can
accept a short downtime.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:27.130
And the downtime will depend
upon the size of the data

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:29.610
that you have in the system.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:32.540
So obviously, at this point,
you stop writing into HBase

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:34.978
during the migration window.

00:13:34.978 --> 00:13:37.270
A slight difference depending
on your type of workload,

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.970
you could leave the system
in a read-only state.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:43.110
Or perhaps you bring down the
entire system for maintenance.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:46.960
You'll then batch copy the
data from HBase into Bigtable.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:49.090
And we have things
like Dataflow jobs

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:52.540
that can read sequence files and
do fast loading into Bigtable

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:53.950
for you.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:57.880
Then you'll want to switch over
your serving path from HBase

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:01.900
to Bigtable, and perhaps do some
calls to check between the two

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:05.710
systems to make sure they're
in a consistent state.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:08.870
At that time, you can exit
your maintenance window

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:12.380
and resume writing, but now
into the Bigtable system.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.860
A more complex
migration is where

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.380
you're migrating with
the system online,

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:19.700
but your data has
a time to live.

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:22.450
And so this is a
common workload pattern

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:24.590
where you're using
Bigtable for something

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:27.160
like more persistent caching.

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:30.670
Or perhaps you have a data
set that has a natural cycle.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:34.030
Perhaps it's updated every
day and recalculated.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.580
So what this allows
you to do is to have

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:39.700
the code that writes updated
to write into both HBase

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.530
and Bigtable with the same
time to live information.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.670
You then simply
wait until the time

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:49.030
to live on all previous data
in the system has expired.

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:52.990
At this point, HBase and Cloud
Bigtable should be in sync.

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.990
You can run verification to make
sure everything's performing

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.180
as expected on the write path.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:01.390
Once you're happy with that,
you can update the read side

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:04.240
to start reading
from Cloud Bigtable,

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:07.420
wait for the information to
finish expiring from HBase

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:08.930
once you start writing into it.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:12.050
And then you should be safe
to decommission your existing

00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:12.550
system.

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:15.790
And you've migrated, without
downtime, onto Bigtable.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:19.840
The last one-- and this
one is more complicated.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.040
This is where you're
migrating a workload that

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.450
has persistent data
without downtime.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:27.840
And so once again,
the first step

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:31.613
here is updating the
write layer of the system.

00:15:31.613 --> 00:15:33.655
But you're also going to
want to look at the read

00:15:33.655 --> 00:15:35.530
layer of the system.

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:40.620
Once you've got the updates
writing to both HBase

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:44.310
and Bigtable, you'll want to
run a verification that that's

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:47.700
happening correctly and start
batch copying all the existing

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:50.690
information that's in Bigtable.

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:52.730
Once you've migrated
all the information,

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:55.130
you're two systems
should be in sync.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.350
You can then migrate
the read workload,

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.180
verify performance
of the system,

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:01.190
and start decommissioning
the old system

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:02.950
at that point in time.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:04.700
Now this one can take
a little bit longer,

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:08.600
because you have to have
both the dual writing

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.880
upfront and then the
backfill operation.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:12.440
So it's sort of a
combination of some

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:14.660
of these previous approaches.

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:19.800
Now perhaps HBase isn't the only
type of workload you've got.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:21.510
So if you've got
Cassandra as well,

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.050
which is also very
similar to Cloud Bigtable,

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:26.090
I highly encourage you
to tune into our session

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:29.310
on migrating Cassandra
workloads into Cloud Bigtable.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.700
And if you've got DynamoDB,
we have another session

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:34.910
that talks about migrating
that into Bigtable and Cloud

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.110
Spanner.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:38.860
You can also watch
Cloud Bigtable in action

00:16:38.860 --> 00:16:42.090
through our showcase demo done
in partnership with Spotify.

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:44.440
I would really encourage
you to look at that demo.

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:46.880
And if you want to get
hands-on and try it yourself,

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.140
we have the quick
labs for Bigtable

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:52.250
that you can go through, get
some hands-on experience.

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:54.710
And there is also
the HBase to Bigtable

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.810
migration guide, which
walks you through some

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:59.030
of the technical steps
here, commands to run,

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:01.080
and tooling that's available.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.230
So I want to thank you for
tuning into this session

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.240
on modernizing
HBase for workloads.

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:07.118
I hope you got
something out of it.

00:17:07.118 --> 00:17:08.660
And I'm looking
forward to seeing you

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:12.040
move to a more managed future.

